On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:37:52 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> The build fix bumps sonic to v1.15.4 and sonic/loader to v0.5.2 with
>> "go mod edit" in post-fetch and post-extract. Both run just before
>> go.mk's download and vendor steps, so no GH_TUPLE list is needed and
>> extract stays offline.
>
> My only concern with this is that it will violate the network rule -
> downloads can only happen in fetch.
>
> Explain to me how this works.

It keeps the rule. Only one step downloads anything, and it runs in
fetch. The two edits never touch the network.

bsd.port.mk runs the fetch phase as _FETCH_SEQ, which puts post-fetch
at 700. go.mk adds go-post-fetch to the same sequence at 800. Both
are fetch targets, so in poudriere both run while the network is up.

1. post-fetch (fetch, 700) works in the go.mod in
   DISTDIR/go/net-mgmt_signoz/signoz-v0.145.0. It copies v0.145.0.mod
   to go.mod and runs "go mod edit" on the copy. That command only
   rewrites the require lines in that file. It copies instead of
   editing in place because go-post-fetch otherwise symlinks go.mod
   to v0.145.0.mod, and an edit through the symlink would change a
   checksummed distfile.

2. go-post-fetch (fetch, 800) is go.mk's unchanged
   "go mod download -x all" against that go.mod, with
   GOPROXY=proxy.golang.org. It fills the module cache under
   DISTDIR/go/net-mgmt_signoz/pkg/mod. It is the only download. The
   bump changes which sonic versions it fetches, not when. As in every
   GO_MODULE port, sum.golang.org verifies the modules, not distinfo,
   and the go command caches those lookups in the same module cache.

3. post-extract (extract, 700) runs the same "go mod edit" on
   WRKSRC/go.mod. It is offline for the same reason as step 1.

4. go-post-extract (extract, 800) is go.mk's unchanged "go mod tidy
   -e" and "go mod vendor -e", run with
   GOPROXY=file://DISTDIR/go/net-mgmt_signoz/pkg/mod/cache/download.
   That proxy is the local cache from step 2, so tidy and vendor read
   only what fetch already downloaded. The build then runs with
   GOPROXY=off and -mod=vendor.

There are two edits because fetch and extract each read their own
go.mod: the one in DISTDIR and the one in WRKSRC. SONIC_BUMP holds
the versions for both, so they cannot drift. If they ever did, tidy
would ask the file:// proxy for a module that is not in the cache.
That fails the build at extract; it does not reach the network.

As the v2 notes say, I ran extract with the network blocked, and
tidy and vendor both succeeded.

